  "query_preview": "..\nMonday through Sunday: A progressive weather pattern this week with \nthe imprints of El Nino having a noticeable influence on storm \nsystems that cross the eastern Pacific. There is an upper level \ntrough moving in over the Inland Northwest early Monday. A frontal \nband is hung up over the eastern portion of the forecast area that \nwill bring persistent light rain to extreme eastern WA and the Idaho \nPanhandle throughout the morning hours. The band is expected to \nweaken with the footprint of where the rain is dwindling into the \nafternoon hours but cloud cover will remain. Further west near the \nCascades, drier air will push in and clear out much of the cloud \ncover. This will yield sunshine once morning fog in the western \nColumbia Basin burns off.\n\nThe next low pressure system begins to push into the western \ncoastline Tuesday night into Wednesday. However, this one like so \nmany that we've seen so far this fall will take a trajectory south \nwith much of the dynamics headed towards Oregon and California. A \nweakening deformation band will bring up to a 50 percent chance for \nprecipitation across the southern portions of the forecast area. Any \nprecipitaiton that does occur will be light. Snow levels fall to \naround 3000 feet and light snow may result in minor travel impacts \nto Stevens Pass and Blewett Pass into Wednesday morning, but not \nmuch more than around an inch of total accumulation is expected.\n\nA third low pressure system for the weekend taking a similar \ntrajectory to the low at the middle of the week will bring a chance \nof precipitation to the region. The model ensembles do indicate a \ntrack that is a bit further north though with all areas having \naround a 40 percent chance of precipitation Saturday into Saturday \nnight. El Nino tends to favor energy diving further south as it \nmoves in off of the eastern Pacific, so I wouldn't be surprised if \nthis trend takes places with the weekend system. It will be a bit \ncolder with snow levels generally between 2000 and 3500 feet. That \nwould mean the potential for at least a rain/snow mix in the low \nlands if we do get the moisture. Temperatures will cool down to\naround normal for mid November by Tuesday with highs generally \nin the upper 30s to low 40s and lows cooling into the upper 20s \nand low 30s. These temperatures look to remain steady through \nthe rest of the week into the weekend. /SVH",
